WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Raise the pressure inside a garden hose, and it bulges.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.620
Raise the pressure inside a small artery, and the muscle in its wall pushes back.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.060
The artery narrows against the stretch.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.800
That answer is built into the muscle, not wired in from the brain.

5
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.140
So what is the muscle sensing, and how does it make force?

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:24.733
This muscle is smooth muscle.

7
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:28.693
It has no troponin, so calcium cannot act on the thin filament.

8
00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:31.553
The switch sits on myosin instead.

9
00:00:32.453 --> 00:00:37.973
Calcium binds calmodulin, and calcium and calmodulin together activate myosin light

10
00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:43.373
chain kinase. The kinase puts a phosphate on the regulatory light chain, and then

11
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:44.953
the motor can cycle on actin.

12
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:48.083
But the kinase is only half the story.

13
00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:53.423
Myosin light chain phosphatase takes the phosphate off, and it works the whole time.

14
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.943
So force follows the balance of kinase and phosphatase.

15
00:00:57.523 --> 00:01:02.523
Calcium sets the kinase, so anything that changes the phosphatase changes force at

16
00:01:02.523 --> 00:01:08.363
the same calcium. That is calcium sensitization: more force at an unchanged calcium

17
00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:09.363
concentration.

18
00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:11.037
Now move the balance.

19
00:01:11.777 --> 00:01:16.517
RhoA activates Rho kinase, and Rho kinase inhibits the phosphatase.

20
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:21.317
More phosphate stays on myosin, so force rises at the same calcium.

21
00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:28.017
The opposite arrow runs through nitric oxide, cGMP, and protein kinase G. In

22
00:01:28.017 --> 00:01:32.317
rabbit artery, the result is a lower calcium and, the authors read, an indirect

23
00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:33.997
activation of the phosphatase.

24
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:39.770
In mouse mesenteric arteries on a pressure myograph, calcium rose with pressure as

25
00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:46.410
normal. Arteries lacking G12 and G13 in smooth muscle showed no active constriction

26
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:52.530
and much less active RhoA. In control arteries, a Rho kinase inhibitor blocked the

27
00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:57.830
response. So this bed needs two things: a calcium rise, and Rho-dependent

28
00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:02.790
sensitization. How the pathways are wired upstream is unsettled.

29
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:07.733
Flow in an idealized rigid arteriole is proportional to the pressure difference

30
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:09.773
times the fourth power of the radius.

31
00:02:10.313 --> 00:02:15.953
Take made-up numbers: 60 millimetres of mercury, and a radius of 100 micrometres.

32
00:02:16.473 --> 00:02:21.193
Raise the difference to 80 millimetres of mercury, and a passive vessel stretched 5

33
00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:23.713
% carries 62 % more flow.

34
00:02:24.293 --> 00:02:31.353
To hold flow constant, the radius must fall to 93.1 micrometres, a 6.9 %

35
00:02:31.353 --> 00:02:36.833
narrowing. Keep one rule: calcium sets the switch, and sensitivity sets the gain.
